---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Getting Started" description="Velora CSS — a motion-native design system. Zero JavaScript animations. Modern CSS only.">
  <span class="vl-kicker">Documentation</span>

  <h1 class="docs-page-title">Getting Started</h1>
  <p class="docs-page-lead docs-page-lead--narrow">
    A motion-native design system. Declarative HTML attributes. Zero JavaScript for animation. Modern CSS only.
  </p>

  <h2>Installation</h2>
  <p>Install the package from the npm registry:</p>
  <pre><code>pnpm add @velora/css
# or
npm install @velora/css</code></pre>

  <p>Then import it once at the root of your app:</p>
  <pre><code>@import "@velora/css";</code></pre>

  <p>Or in a bundler entrypoint:</p>
  <pre><code>import "@velora/css";</code></pre>

  <h2>Quick Start</h2>
  <p>Add motion to any element with two attributes:</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;!-- Fade up when scrolled into view --&gt;
&lt;div vl-effect="fade-up" vl-timeline="view"&gt;
  Reveals on scroll
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- Staggered children entrance --&gt;
&lt;section vl-children="stagger" vl-stagger="100ms"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Child 1&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Child 2&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;Child 3&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;!-- Full scene orchestration --&gt;
&lt;section
  vl-scene="hero"
  vl-effect="scene-hero-reveal"
  vl-timeline="view"
  vl-range="entry 0% cover 70%"
  vl-pin&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Heading fades up&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;img src="hero.jpg" alt="" /&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Copy blurs in&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;a href="#" class="vl-cta"&gt;Get started&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>Page Transitions</h2>
  <p>Enable MPA cross-document view transitions with one attribute:</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;!-- In your root layout --&gt;
&lt;html vl-page-transition="cinema"&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Requires <code>@view-transition &#123; navigation: auto; &#125;</code> in your CSS (already included with Velora).
    Supported in Chrome 111+, Edge 111+.
  </p>

  <h2>Philosophy</h2>
  <p>
    Velora is a <strong>declarative motion system for HTML</strong> — not a UI kit, not a JavaScript animation library.
  </p>
  <p>The core idea is a three-step contract:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write <strong>semantic HTML</strong></li>
    <li>Add <strong><code>vl-*</code> attributes</strong> to declare intent</li>
    <li>Velora maps those to <strong>CSS-based motion, layout, and transitions</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The framework proves that modern HTML and CSS are sufficient to build premium motion systems when the
    architecture is designed correctly.
  </p>

  <h2>CSS Layer Stack</h2>
  <p>Velora organizes CSS into 8 cascade layers, making it safe to override at any point:</p>
  <pre><code>@layer velora.reset,
       velora.tokens,
       velora.layout,
       velora.motion,
       velora.components,
       velora.transitions,
       velora.utilities,
       velora.overrides;</code></pre>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Layer</th>
        <th>Contents</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>velora.reset</code></td><td>Browser normalization and base adjustments</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.tokens</code></td><td>Colors, spacing, typography, motion tokens</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.layout</code></td><td>Container, stack, grid, and scene wrappers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.motion</code></td><td>Effect presets, timelines, stagger, reveals</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.components</code></td><td>Cards, buttons, navbars, accordions, badges</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.transitions</code></td><td>Page transitions and shared element rules</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.utilities</code></td><td>Low-level opt-in utility classes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>velora.overrides</code></td><td>Last-mile overrides; highest specificity</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    All Velora motion is <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> aware. Users who have requested reduced motion
    see instant transitions with no animation.
  </p>
  <pre><code>/* Built into every Velora effect */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) &#123;
  [vl-effect],
  [vl-scene],
  [vl-page-transition] &#123;
    animation: none !important;
    transition-duration: 0ms !important;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

